.TH prtroot 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
prtroot \- find which device the root is on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prtroot
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-i\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B prtroot
looks through
.B /dev
for the block device whose number matches the device the root is mounted
from, and prints it.  This is how a boot script finds out where it
booted from, which the kernel does not otherwise say.
.PP
By default it prints the device followed by
.B " / fuzix rw 0 0"
\- a complete
.B /etc/mtab
line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Print just the device name.
.TP
.B \-i
Write the line to
.B /etc/mtab
instead of to standard output, replacing whatever was there.
.B /etc/rc
does this at boot so that
.BR mount (1)
and
.BR df (1)
have something to read.
.SH NOTES
If no matching device node exists it prints
.BR /dev/unknown ,
which is a sign that
.B /dev
is missing a node rather than that anything is wrong with the root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR substroot (1),
.BR mount (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR remount (1)
